# Backend Rules (Rust / Tauri)

## Stack
- Tauri v2
- `aws-sdk-s3` for S3-compatible operations (R2, MinIO, Wasabi, B2)
- `tokio` for async runtime
- `serde` / `serde_json` for serialization
- `thiserror` for typed errors
- `keyring` for secure credential storage

---

## Project Structure

```
src-tauri/src/
├── main.rs          # Entry point — calls lib::run(), do not modify
├── lib.rs           # App setup: .invoke_handler(), plugin registration, state setup
├── commands/
│   ├── mod.rs       # Re-exports all command modules
│   ├── bucket.rs    # list_files, delete_file, create_folder, get_presigned_url
│   ├── upload.rs    # upload_file, upload_stream, multipart handling
│   ├── download.rs  # download_file, stream_to_disk
│   └── connection.rs # add_connection, test_connection, list_connections, remove_connection
├── s3/
│   ├── mod.rs       # Re-exports
│   ├── client.rs    # S3 client builder — resolves credentials and endpoint by provider
│   ├── operations.rs# Raw S3 ops (list, put, delete, presign) — no Tauri types here
│   └── types.rs     # S3-specific types (BucketFile, UploadProgress, etc.)
├── storage/
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── credentials.rs # keyring read/write for access keys
│   └── connections.rs # persist connection configs (sans secrets) to app data dir
├── error.rs         # AppError enum + impl Into<String> for Tauri command returns
└── state.rs         # Tauri managed state (S3ClientCache, ActiveConnection, etc.)
```

### Rules
- No file over 500 lines — split into a submodule and re-export
- No business logic in `lib.rs` or `main.rs`
- Commands in `commands/` are thin: validate input → call `s3/` or `storage/` → map error → return
- Raw S3 API calls live in `s3/operations.rs` only — nothing else touches the SDK directly

---

## Credential storage rules

- Secret access keys are ONLY stored via `storage::credentials` (keyring crate).
- `ConnectionConfig` MUST NOT contain `secret_access_key` — ever.
- App data dir JSON contains config only — safe to read in logs/debug.
- Keyring failures map to `AppError::Keyring` — never silently swallow them.
- R2 endpoint normalization happens in `s3/client.rs` `normalize_r2_endpoint()` — nowhere else.
- Connection test MUST succeed before any credentials are persisted.

---

## Command Structure

Commands are the only Tauri-exposed layer. They must:
1. Accept only serializable types
2. Return `Result<T, String>` where the error is from `AppError`
3. Delegate immediately to a service function — no logic inside the command fn itself

```rust
// commands/bucket.rs
use tauri::State;
use crate::state::AppState;
use crate::s3::operations;
use crate::s3::types::BucketFile;
use crate::error::AppError;

#[tauri::command]
pub async fn list_files(
    prefix: String,
    state: State<'_, AppState>,
) -> Result<Vec<BucketFile>, String> {
    let client = state.client().await.map_err(AppError::into_string)?;
    operations::list_objects(&client, &state.active_bucket(), &prefix)
        .await
        .map_err(AppError::into_string)
}

#[tauri::command]
pub async fn delete_file(
    key: String,
    state: State<'_, AppState>,
) -> Result<(), String> {
    let client = state.client().await.map_err(AppError::into_string)?;
    operations::delete_object(&client, &state.active_bucket(), &key)
        .await
        .map_err(AppError::into_string)
}
```

Register all commands in `lib.rs`:

```rust
// lib.rs
mod commands;
mod s3;
mod storage;
mod error;
mod state;

use commands::{bucket, upload, download, connection};

#[cfg_attr(mobile, tauri::mobile_entry_point)]
pub fn run() {
    tauri::Builder::default()
        .manage(state::AppState::default())
        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![
            bucket::list_files,
            bucket::delete_file,
            bucket::create_folder,
            upload::upload_file,
            download::download_file,
            connection::add_connection,
            connection::test_connection,
            connection::list_connections,
            connection::remove_connection,
        ])
        .run(tauri::generate_context!())
        .expect("error running tauri app");
}
```

